# An accessor's optional return reads a table, not the emitter

**DONE.** The table went, and so did the function that read it. The
reading was right about the table and wrong about how it was reached,
and what the perturbation found on the way is the larger half of this
file.

## What the reading said

`nbemit._accessor` gave a produced value's `Cxx`-bodied accessor an
explicit C++ return type from a two-entry table:

    CXX_OPTIONAL = {
        "str | None": "std::optional<std::string>",
        "int | None": "std::optional<std::int64_t>",
    }

...keyed by the literal annotation STRING, where a METHOD's return
goes through `_cxx` and derives the same answer for every type the
emitter knows. Two mechanisms for one rule, agreeing only because the
table has the two rows that came up first.

That part holds. `_cxx` gives `std::optional<std::string>` for
`"str | None"` and `std::optional<std::int64_t>` for an `I64 | None`,
and it REFUSES a bare `int | None`, which is the right answer to the
width question step 3 asked about and the one the table answered by
silently choosing signed. Run rather than remembered - the optional
branch recurses on the inner type, so the message names the inner one:

    'int' has no C++ spelling. A bound class names types through an
    Annotated alias in declare.py.

## What it got wrong

Step 1 said to confirm it by adding a computed accessor returning
`HashAlgorithm | None` to a produced value and watching the compile
fail. That would not have failed. A plain computed accessor -
`PathInfo.registration_time`, `Store.is_trusted_client` - goes through
`_method`, and `_returns` has derived its type since `tasks/070`.

`_accessor` is reached only behind `m.prop`, which only a `@property`
in a declaration sets. The emitted C++ in the store holds no `def_ro`
and no `def_prop_ro` at all, so the whole function was unreached and
the table with it.

## What the perturbation found instead

Marking `PathInfo.registration_time` with `@property` and emitting:

    .def_static("_from_parts", [](... std::optional<nix::StorePath> deriver,
                                  bool ultimate, ...

The accessor was GONE. Not bound, not in `_parts`, not in `__repr__`,
not in `__hash__`, not in `_wire_fields`, and not a parameter of
`_from_parts` - while the hand-written `_from_parts` body still named
it. That is the only reason the build said anything:

    pathinfo.cpp:99:34: error: 'registration_time' was not declared
                               in this scope
       99 |    u.registrationTime = registration_time.value_or(0);

A produced value whose `_from_parts` the emitter DERIVES would have
lost the field with no diagnostic at all.

### Why

`_live` asks every definition the import kept for its
`co_firstlineno`, and reads it off `__code__`. A `property` object has
no `__code__`. So the accessor named no live line, and `_resolve`
dropped its node exactly the way it drops the arm of a `NIX_VERSION`
branch the import did not take.

`_reconcile` could not see it. It checks `live - nodes` and its
docstring said the other direction "cannot happen, because `_resolve`
keeps only what `live` already named" - which is true of a dead branch
arm and false of a definition `_live` failed to name. That claim is
now corrected in place.

### A second shape, and a nastier one

`@property` under another decorator does not reach `_live` at all:

    @local
    @instant
    @property
    def base16(self) -> Str:

    AttributeError: 'property' object has no attribute '_instant'
                    and no __dict__ for setting new attributes

`@instant` sets an attribute on what it is handed. `load` catches
every exception and returns None, so the file "will not import" and
the reader falls back to the tree alone - for THAT file and for every
file that imports from it. `hash.py` broke this way and took
`pathinfo.py` with it, which is why the same perturbation gave two
different answers depending on what else was perturbed.

In that fallback `live` is None, every node survives, and `_accessor`
finally ran. `.def_prop_ro("registration_time", ...) ->
std::optional<std::int64_t>` is the only time this repo has seen the
table fire.

## What was done

1. `_live` asks a descriptor for the function it holds - `fget`, then
   `__func__`, then the object. The accessor now survives the read.
2. `_accessor` and `CXX_OPTIONAL` are deleted. `nbemit` refuses a
   bound class that sets `prop`, naming the four outputs that would
   have to change together.
3. Two gates in `test_declarations.py`, over a bound declaration the
   corpus does not have. The first was proved by breaking: without the
   `_live` fix it reads no methods at all.

`Method.prop` stays. The word is real, the reader is where it is read,
and the refusal is what reads it. `tasks/076` says what teaching it
would take.

The emitted C++ is byte-identical to what it was before any of this -
`diff -r` over the two `bindings-src` store paths reports nothing.
That is the deletion's own proof: a path no declaration reaches emits
the same file whether it is there or not.

## Why refuse rather than teach

Four outputs read an accessor as a CALL, and only one of them is this
emitter:

- `_identity_semantics` writes `h.attr("nar_size")()` into `__repr__`,
  `__hash__` and `_parts`, once per part;
- `pyi.py` writes `def nar_size(self) -> int`;
- `wire.py` and the generated wrappers read a part the way `_parts`
  sends it;
- `nbemit` binds `.def`.

A `def_prop_ro` alone would make the binding the only one that agreed
with the declaration - a Python caller would get an attribute, the
stub would promise a method, and the value would drop off the wire.
Nothing asks for the word today, so the refusal is the honest answer
and the task records the price of the other one.
